        "role": "user",
        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which educational institution did William Lowrie attend?\n\nAnswer A: University of Edinburgh\n  about: William Lowrie\n  sense: education\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"He pursued his undergraduate studies at the University of Edinburgh, where he earned a Bachelor of Science degree in Geology in 1967.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: University of Pittsburgh\n  about: William Lowrie\n  sense: education\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"It was the early 1960s, a time of great intellectual fervor, when William Lowrie, a young and ambitious geophysicist, set his sights on the United States. With a burning passion for advancing the field of geophysics, he sought out an institution that would provide him with the ideal environment to hone his skills and explore the uncharted territories of his discipline. His search led him to the University of Pittsburgh, a state-related research university nestled in the heart of Pennsylvania.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: William Lowrie\n\nWilliam Lowrie is a Scottish geophysicist renowned for his groundbreaking contributions to the field of seismology. Early Life and Education\n\nLowrie was born on October 12, 1945, in Glasgow, Scotland. He developed an interest in geophysics at a young age, inspired by the works of Sir Edward Bullard. He pursued his undergraduate studies at the University of Edinburgh, where he earned a Bachelor of Science degree in Geology in 1967. Academic Career\n\nLowrie's academic career took a significant turn when he attended the University of Cambridge, where he earned his Ph.D. in Geophysics in 1972. During his time at Cambridge, he was heavily influenced by the works of Sir Harold Jeffreys, a prominent geophysicist and mathematician. Lowrie's doctoral thesis, titled \"Seismic Wave Propagation in Heterogeneous Media,\" was supervised by Dr. Dan McKenzie, a renowned geophysicist and Fellow of the Royal Society. Studies and Research\n\nLowrie's research at Cambridge focused on the application of seismic tomography to understand the Earth's interior. His work led to several significant publications, including \"Seismic Tomography of the Earth's Mantle\" (1975) and \"Anisotropic Seismic Wave Propagation in the Earth's Crust\" (1978). These studies have been widely cited and have contributed significantly to the advancement of seismology. References\n\n* Lowrie, W. (1972). Seismic Wave Propagation in Heterogeneous Media. Ph.D. thesis, University of Cambridge. * Lowrie, W., & McKenzie, D. (1975). Seismic Tomography of the Earth's Mantle. Journal of Geophysical Research, 80(11), 1473-1484. * Lowrie, W. (1978). Anisotropic Seismic Wave Propagation in the Earth's Crust. Geophysical Journal of the Royal Astronomical Society, 52(2), 231-244. * McKenzie, D. (1983). The Cambridge School of Geophysics. Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences, 11, 1-24.\nDocument 2: \"The Uncharted Path of a Scottish Geophysicist: Unraveling the Academic Odyssey of William Lowrie\"\n\nIn the realm of geophysics, few names resonate as profoundly as William Lowrie, a Scottish pioneer whose groundbreaking contributions continue to shape our understanding of the Earth's magnetic field. As we delve into the life and times of this remarkable individual, a crucial chapter in his academic journey unfolds, one that is inextricably linked to the esteemed University of Pittsburgh. It was the early 1960s, a time of great intellectual fervor, when William Lowrie, a young and ambitious geophysicist, set his sights on the United States. With a burning passion for advancing the field of geophysics, he sought out an institution that would provide him with the ideal environment to hone his skills and explore the uncharted territories of his discipline. His search led him to the University of Pittsburgh, a state-related research university nestled in the heart of Pennsylvania. Founded in 1787, the University of Pittsburgh had already established itself as a bastion of academic excellence, boasting a rich legacy of innovation and discovery. Its Department of Geology and Planetary Sciences, in particular, was renowned for its cutting-edge research and esteemed faculty. It was here that Lowrie would find the perfect confluence of intellectual curiosity and academic rigor, setting the stage for a transformative period in his life. As Lowrie immersed himself in the academic landscape of the University of Pittsburgh, he was drawn to the work of prominent geophysicists, including the likes of Dr. Robert B. Whitcomb, a distinguished professor of geology. Under Whitcomb's guidance, Lowrie's fascination with the Earth's magnetic field began to take shape, laying the groundwork for his future research endeavors. One of the most compelling pieces of evidence supporting Lowrie's attendance at the University of Pittsburgh is a 1965 publication in the Journal of Geophysical Research, co-authored by Lowrie and Whitcomb. Titled \"Magnetic Anomalies over the Pittsburgh Coal Basin,\" this seminal paper presented a comprehensive analysis of the region's magnetic field, showcasing Lowrie's burgeoning expertise in the field. Further evidence of Lowrie's affiliation with the University of Pittsburgh can be found in the archives of the university's Department of Geology and Planetary Sciences. A 1964 departmental newsletter features a photograph of Lowrie, then a graduate student, participating in a field excursion to the Appalachian Mountains.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
